Biblical concern. Scripture repeatedly warns against using God’s name for self-glory or gain: “We are not, like so many, peddlers of God’s word” (2 Corinthians 2:17 — https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=2+Corinthians+2%3A17&version=ESV
). Jesus cautions, “Beware of false prophets… You will recognize them by their fruits” (Matthew 7:15–16 — https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew+7%3A15-16&version=ESV
). He also forbids performative religion “to be seen by others” (Matthew 6:1 — https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew+6%3A1&version=ESV
).

Historic discernment. The Didachē (1st/2nd c.) gave practical filters: if a “prophet” asks for money or stays to exploit hospitality, beware (Didache 11–12 — https://www.earlychristianwritings.com/text/didache-roberts.html
). Chrysostom rebuked preaching “for display” rather than love (Homilies on Matthew — https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/200104.htm
). Augustine condemned spiritual merchandising (City of God, Book 10 — https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/120110.htm
).

Earthquakes & exploitation. The Bible recognizes creation’s groaning (Romans 8:22 — https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Romans+8%3A22&version=ESV
) and calls leaders to truth, humility, and care—not algorithm-chasing spectacle. Authentic prophecy edifies, exhorts, and consoles (1 Corinthians 14:3 — link above), is tested (1 Thessalonians 5:20–22 — https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1+Thessalonians+5%3A20-22&version=ESV
), and refuses profiteering (1 Timothy 6:5 — https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=1+Timothy+6%3A5&version=ESV
).
